apers today that  annelise dodds could lose her position  as shadow chancellor  and be sacked in a reshuffle you a  little bit relieved you've been put up  for the morning round this morning  perhaps  saying or might be safe in your position  i don't take any notice of the gossip  and the speculation that appears in the  newspapers around this it's something  that  part of political life annalisa dodds  i've been working with over the past uh  12 months  is doing an excellent job and as a very  strong team  led by kierstarma we are the ones who  are putting forward the very clear  choice of this election  of a labour party that's on the side of  communities recovering from the  coronavirus  around the country in contrast to i'm  afraid a conservative party that just  wants to take this country back to the  past  a politician not interested in reshuffle  speculation that must be a first  nick thomas simmons thank you very much  for being on the program  now before we move on quick reminder do  check out the safety rage on sunday  podcast we record it straight after the  show and this week of course we're going  to be discussing the developments in  scotland  amongst other issues and you're going to  be able to listen to that from lunchtime  on the sky news app spotify apple  podcast wherever you  usually find your podcasts now this week  china  imposed sanctions on 10 uk organizations  and individuals for spreading what it  calls  lies and disinformation about human  rights abuses now the sanctions hit five  conservative mps  including the former minister mr ghani  who joins us now and  what happened well it's a curious state  of affairs sophie a number of us were  sanctioned and i think  that chinese communist parties thought  that they were intimidating us or were  going to stop us talking about  gross human rights abuses particularly  in xinjiang but it's been a complete  misstep by the chinese communist party  it's done a couple of things instead  it's actually made parliament come  together  and say we won't have democratically  elected officials intimidated anyway  it's also safe given us an opportunity  to talk about the one thing  the chinese communist party do not want  us to talk about which is the gross  human rights abuses against the uyghur  people and the fact that a genocide has  been called out  against china on the way that they are  treating the weaker people by many  countries around the world  you of course spoken out very strongly  about some of those human rights  abuses i just wonder if you feel that  the uk government has been tough enough  in its dealings with china  if you look at for example the  integrated defense review published a  couple of weeks ago  it calls for a positive trade and  investment relationship  with china you know given the treatment  of the uyghurs given the situation in  hong kong given the fact this is a  country that has sanctioned  british mps is that really good enough  well we must remember these sanctions  are in retaliation for the uk government  sanctioning chinese officials who have  been  implicated in the gross human rights  abuses so the uk government is  definitely in the right direction we  would ask many of us colleagues  will ask that the uk government goes  even further and sanctions even more  chinese officials who are involved in  the abuses in xinjiang  and what's also come about because of  the sanctions sophie  a greater discussion about where we  stand  in the new global order if the chinese  communist party is going to be using  both its  financial power through its belt and  road initiative and also using these  so-called sanctions to try and silence  individuals like myself i think these  sanctions are a turning point in how we  consider our long-term relationship  with china but not just us individually  just the uk but also working with our  partners countries like america for  example  i think this is a moment that i don't  expect the chinese communist party  wanted to come about  because of the sanctions but we now have  an opportunity to think about  are we going to assert our values and  how we're going to do that  now boris johnson hosted the mps who had  been sanctioned  yesterday in downing street couldn't  help but notice that tom tuggenheim  wasn't there the  chair of the foreign affairs select  committee who also was sanctioned  um he tweeted to say he wasn't invited  do you know what happened  the meeting was organized very quickly  at the last minute because the sanctions  came about over  overnight on thursday we were made aware  of them on friday and the meeting came  about very quickly  on on saturday but look all of us  colleagues  were sanctioned for doing what we've  been elected to do  which so was he invited do you know we  were all invited at very  very um last minute sophie but what's  what's critical here is that we met with  the prime minister and the prime  minister standing  side by side with us which i don't think  was the outcome the chinese communist  party thought they were going to achieve  with the sanctions i assume they thought  that we would be  marginalized by our party and that we  will then feel intimidated in talking  about the abuse of the uyghur  but that definitely is not what has  happened just feels a bit strange you  know  that he tweeted to say he wasn't invited  he wasn't there he's obviously one of  the most high profile  critics of china and the uyghurs you  know it'd be a pretty bad look obviously  for the prime minister to be speaking  at life with free speech but not  inviting tom tugan  perhaps because of things that he said  so for the best of your knowledge  that's not the case he was invited  people were invited at very last minutes  i mean another  parliamentary colleague was in  leicestershire wasn't able to get down  in time um but the story here sophie is  that  we are not going to be intimidated we're  continuing to speak about the abuse of  the weaker 